description:
Location: rust/lichen-rpl/src/dodag.rs lines 169-176 (path_cost)

Issue: The path_cost function checks for NaN and negative values but not infinity:

if link_cost.is_nan() || link_cost < 0.0 {
    return u16::MAX;
}

When link_etx is very large (e.g., f32::MAX), multiplying by mhri can produce f32::INFINITY. This works correctly because Rust 1.45+ saturates infinity-to-integer casts to the max value, but the behavior is implicit rather than explicit.

The comment says NaN or negative -> saturate to max but infinity also needs this treatment.

Impact: Low - works correctly due to Rust saturation semantics, but relies on language-version-specific behavior.

Fix: Make the infinity handling explicit for clarity and defense-in-depth:

if link_cost.is_nan() || link_cost < 0.0 || link_cost.is_infinite() {
    return u16::MAX;
}
